      <h2>5. The Staff Federation Benchmark: The 2.86x Aykroyd Model</h2>
      <p>
        The primary demand submitted by the National Council of the Joint Consultative Machinery (JCM) and major employee confederations centers on a minimum fitment factor of <strong>2.86x</strong>. 
      </p>
      <p>
        This proposal is derived from the <strong>Dr. Wallace Aykroyd Nutritional Formula</strong>, adopted by the 15th Indian Labour Conference (ILC):
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Nutritional Intake:</strong> 2,700 calories per adult consumption unit per day, evaluated across wholesale and retail price indices for balanced food baskets (cereals, pulses, milk, vegetables, edible oil, and sugar).</li>
        <li><strong>Clothing Requirements:</strong> A statutory consumption standard of 72 yards of fabric per year for a standard family unit of three consumption units.</li>
        <li><strong>Housing & Social Contingencies:</strong> 10% rent allowance plus 25% allocation for children’s education, healthcare emergencies, recreation, and social obligations, as affirmed by the Supreme Court in the <em>Reptakos Brett</em> judgment.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Under modern commodity pricing, this nutritional minimum wage computation yields an entry basic floor of approximately <strong>₹51,480 per month</strong> for Level 1 (MTS/Group D), which translates directly to a 2.86x multiplier on the 7th CPC baseline of ₹18,000.
      </p>

      <h2>6. The Fiscal Counterbalance: Revenue Deficit and Macroeconomic Impact</h2>
      <p>
        While employee confederations emphasize cost-of-living increases, the Ministry of Finance must weigh the broader fiscal impact on the union budget:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Direct Union Wage Bill:</strong> Central civil ministries, defence establishments, and Indian Railways employ over 4.8 million active personnel and disburse pensions to more than 6.7 million retirees.</li>
        <li><strong>The State Government Ripple Effect:</strong> Individual state governments traditionally follow Central Pay Commission recommendations by adopting matching pay scales and fitment ratios. A high central multiplier strains state fiscal deficits, prompting recommendations for a more balanced baseline of <strong>2.25x to 2.57x</strong>.</li>
      </ul>

